{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And it was on the morrow</b> — on the morrow of the day on which they did what was mentioned above, Moses sat to judge the people. They said in the Mekhilta (here): on the morrow of the Day of Atonement. But their intent cannot be that מממחרת is an allusion to the Day of Atonement, for the Day of Atonement is not mentioned in Scripture such that it should say about it ממחרת. And likewise the intention cannot be that it was literally the morrow of it, for they did not eat on the Day of Atonement — if they even had a Day of Atonement in the first year before they were commanded concerning it. Furthermore, on the Day of Atonement the latter tablets were given, and on its morrow Moses came down and spoke with the children of Israel and commanded them all that HaShem had spoken with him on Mount Sinai (Exodus 34:32), and that is not a day of judgment on which the people would stand from morning until evening. And moreover it is impossible that this was in the second year on the Day of Atonement, for when the standards journeyed he said, \"for I am going to my land and to my kindred\" (Numbers 10:30). Rather, the intent of this baraita is to say that this was after the Day of Atonement, for they had no free day for judgment from the day they came to Mount Sinai until after the Day of Atonement of this first year." "And he said, <b>a burnt-offering and sacrifices to Elohim</b> — because Jethro did not yet know YHWH, for Moses said, <b>\"what YHWH did to Pharaoh and to Egypt… and YHWH delivered them\"</b> (Exodus 18:8), but Jethro offered to Elohim. And thus this expression is not found in any of the sacrifices in the Torah of the priests, as I will explain with the help of Heaven (Leviticus 1:9). And likewise <b>\"when the people come to me to inquire of Elohim\"</b>, <b>\"and I make known to them the statutes of Elohim</b> (verses 15–16 below) — the words of Moses to his father-in-law. And it is possible that he said this to him because <b>\"for judgment is Elohim's\"</b> (Deuteronomy 1:17), just as our Sages always interpret: Elohim — this is the attribute of judgment (Bereshit Rabbah 73:3)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {1 "The phrase 'כי המשפט לאלהים הוא' is cited from Deuteronomy 1:17; Ramban reads it in light of the rabbinic identification of the divine name Elohim with the attribute of strict justice."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:10:37.157727417Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}